//? Title: Lagrange interpolation through actual products
//? Subtitle: Exact small controls, original Runge oscillations and node conditioning
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given values at distinct coordinates, interpolation constructs a polynomial that
passes through them; passing through the nodes does not guarantee an accurate
approximation between them. We inspect actual direct-product terms on a
hand-checkable quadratic, retain the original eleven equispaced Runge nodes, and
compare their sampled error with Chebyshev–Lobatto placement. The plots include
the full observed overshoot and distinguish mathematical interpolation,
approximation conditioning and finite-arithmetic evaluation limits.
)

\toc

> The direct polynomial and its limits

For n distinct nodes, the Lagrange basis is

$$ L_j(x)=\prod_{k\ne j}\frac{x-x_k}{x_j-x_k},\qquad p(x)=\sum_j y_jL_j(x). $$
\alttext(Each basis polynomial is a product of ratios over the other nodes; the interpolating polynomial is their value-weighted sum.)

At a node x_i, L_j(x_i) is one if i=j and zero otherwise. Hence p interpolates
the values. Uniqueness follows because the difference of two degree-at-most n−1
interpolants would have n distinct roots. This does not say p is close to a
different underlying function between nodes. For sufficiently smooth f the
exact-arithmetic remainder involves f’s nth derivative and the product of
distances to all nodes, so node placement and function smoothness matter.

The native API evaluates this direct product in original j-then-k order and
sequentially adds its terms. It is not a barycentric evaluator or coefficient
solver. Complete shape/finite/distinct-node checks precede evaluation. Ordinary
finite subtraction/division order is preserved; when a double subtraction
overflows, both differences and their ratio are recomputed in long double, then
the ratio rounded once. Every ratio, product and sum must still be finite
double. A true finite polynomial value can therefore be rejected if a direct
intermediate overflows; no general stability or error certificate is promised.

> A quadratic you can check by hand

Use x=−1,0,1, y=x²+1 and query0.5. The basis weights are−0.125,0.75,0.375 and
weighted terms−0.25,0.75,0.75, giving1.25. The following observations are actual
producing products and sums, not a separate basis reconstruction.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lagrange_terms.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::lagrange_trace({-1, 0, 1}, {2, 1, 2}, .5);
    std::print("| actual term j | factor k | actual ratio | product before | product after |\n| ---: | ---: " "| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &e : t.factors)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n", e.term_index,
                   e.excluded_node, e.ratio, e.term_before, e.term_after);
    std::print("\n| actual node term | final contribution | sum before | sum after |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: " "| ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &e : t.terms)
        std::print("| {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n", e.node, e.value,
                   e.sum_before, e.sum_after);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < t.terms.size(); ++j)
        d.node(std::to_string(j), {0, double(1 - int(j)) * 2},
               std::format("Actual term {}\n{}", j, t.terms[j].value));
    d.node("sum", {7, 0}, std::format("Actual sequential value\n{}", t.value));
    for (int j = 0; j < 3; ++j)
        d.edge(std::to_string(j), "sum");
    d.save_svg("build/lagrange_terms.svg");
}
```
