> Node placement changes the approximation problem

Compare eleven Chebyshev–Lobatto nodes cos(pi*i/10) with the original eleven
equispaced nodes, using the same function and2001 evaluation positions.
Endpoints are retained in both sets. The plotted absolute errors are actual
native evaluations, not a general claim that one grid dominates for every
function. Changing node placement changes the interpolating polynomial; changing
to a barycentric evaluation formula would instead evaluate the same polynomial
more efficiently/stably.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lagrange_nodes.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto f = [](double x) { return 1 / (1 + 25 * x * x); };
    std::vector<double> a, b, ya, yb;
    for (int i = 0; i < 11; ++i) {
        a.push_back(-1. + 2. * i / 10);
        b.push_back(std::cos(std::numbers::pi * i / 10.));
        ya.push_back(f(a.back()));
        yb.push_back(f(b.back()));
    }
    std::vector<double> x, ea, eb;
    double ma = 0, mb = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i <= 2000; ++i) {
        double q = -1. + 2. * i / 2000;
        x.push_back(q);
        ea.push_back(std::abs(dm::algorithms::lagrange_interpolate(a, ya, q) -
                              f(q)));
        eb.push_back(std::abs(dm::algorithms::lagrange_interpolate(b, yb, q) -
                              f(q)));
        ma = std::max(ma, ea.back());
        mb = std::max(mb, eb.back());
    }
    std::print("| actual node placement | nodes | grid size | maximum sampled absolute error |\n| --- | " "---: | ---: | ---: |\n| equispaced | 11 | {} | {:.12e} |\n| Chebyshev-Lobatto | 11 | {} | " "{:.12e} |\n",
               x.size(), ma, x.size(), mb);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, ea, "equispaced", {220, 38, 38}, 2).line(x, eb, "Lobatto",
                                                       {37, 99, 235}, 2).y_limits(-.05, ma * 1.3).title("Same function and degree, different actual nodes").x_label("x").y_label("actual sampled absolute approximation error").save_svg("build/lagrange_nodes.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual node placement | nodes | grid size | maximum sampled absolute error |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| equispaced | 11 | 2001 | 1.915643050219e+00 |
| Chebyshev-Lobatto | 11 | 2001 | 1.321964324367e-01 |
\image(figures/lagrange_nodes.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual same-degree errors for equispaced and Chebyshev–Lobatto nodes.)
\alttext(Computed absolute errors compare two different interpolating polynomials on the same full grid; no fabricated error floor is used.)

> Sampled error versus increasing degree

More equally spaced nodes need not improve the approximation to Runge’s
function. Run complete native evaluations at every grid position for each listed
node count and both placements. Positive measured maximum errors are plotted
logarithmically without replacing zero by an arbitrary floor. This is a finite
study, not a continuous supremum bound or an asymptotic proof. The direct
producer performs n(n−1) ratios/multiplications and n final term additions per
evaluation, excluding validation and copying. Duplicate checks also cost O(n²);
no precomputed barycentric weights are used.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lagrange_degree.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto f = [](double x) { return 1 / (1 + 25 * x * x); };
    std::vector<double> x, e, c;
    std::print("| actual nodes | actual evaluation grid | equispaced maximum sampled error | Lobatto " "maximum sampled error | actual ratios per evaluation |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: " "|\n");
    for (int n : {5, 9, 11, 15, 21, 31, 41}) {
        double errors[2]{};
        std::size_t ratios = 0;
        for (int kind = 0; kind < 2; ++kind) {
            std::vector<double> nodes, values;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
                double q =
                    kind ? std::cos(std::numbers::pi * i / double(n - 1))
                    : -1. + 2. * i / (n - 1);
                nodes.push_back(q);
                values.push_back(f(q));
            }
            for (int i = 0; i <= 2000; ++i) {
                double q = -1. + 2. * i / 2000;
                const auto a =
                    dm::algorithms::lagrange_analyze(nodes, values, q);
                errors[kind] = std::max(errors[kind], std::abs(a.value - f(q)));
                ratios = a.work.ratio_evaluations;
            }
        }
        std::print("| {} | 2001 | {:.12e} | {:.12e} | {} |\n", n, errors[0],
                   errors[1], ratios);
        if (errors[0] <= 0 || errors[1] <= 0)
            throw std::runtime_error("zero error requires separate display");
        x.push_back(n);
        e.push_back(std::log10(errors[0]));
        c.push_back(std::log10(errors[1]));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, e, "equispaced", {220, 38, 38}, 2).points(x, e, "", {220, 38, 38},
                                                        4).line(x, c, "Lobatto",
                                                                {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, c, "", {37, 99, 235}, 4).title("Complete Runge grid studies as degree increases").x_label("actual node count").y_label("log10(maximum sampled absolute error)").save_svg("build/lagrange_degree.svg");
}
```
