\caption(Actual complete sampled-error studies, with no synthetic floor or invented work.)
\alttext(Increasing equispaced degree and Chebyshev–Lobatto degree produce different sampled errors; ratio counts come from the producing native analysis.)

> Scope and verification

Finite query/data, matched shapes and pairwise distinct coordinates are checked
completely before evaluation or capture. Empty input retains the historical zero
convention. The trace owns original x/y values, every actual factor product and
every term/sum boundary; no interpolation replay constructs records. The
complete n*n+2*n count is admitted before copies/history allocation with a
million-entry default and zero disabling only the optional quota. Counts exclude
scalar fields, capacities and bytes/RAM. Conservative finite-intermediate
rejection is distinct from an approximation or conditioning guarantee.

Independent Fraction references checked100 direct evaluations,2724 actual factor
records and546 term/sum records. All28014 evaluations in the14 complete Runge
grids were compared with an independent80-digit Decimal barycentric evaluation
of the same represented nodes/values. The largest observed absolute discrepancy
was about1.29e-6 on the high-degree ill-conditioned controls; relative to the
absolute weighted-term scale, the largest discrepancy was about1.03e-15. These
are observations, not certified rounding bounds. Another5000 ordinary
evaluations matched the frozen direct arithmetic exactly. Native controls verify
the full original overshoot, polynomial reproduction, extrapolation, complete
guards/quotas and ownership. The ten-report shared build passed141 native CTest
cases,45 directly instrumented address/undefined-behaviour sanitizer cases,90
compiled/executed public examples,412/412 scoped file definitions plus11/11
changed lagged-Fibonacci engine definitions, and ten representative clangd
hovers. All four complete report programs executed and all four native figures
were visually reviewed. Receipts and repeatable checks are retained under
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-184-193/`.

> References

J.-P. Berrut and L. N. Trefethen(2004),
[Barycentric Lagrange Interpolation|https://doi.org/10.1137/S0036144502417715],
SIAM Review46(3),501–517. The paper explains interpolation forms and node
conditioning; this report explicitly uses the native direct-product evaluator
rather than attributing barycentric properties to it.
